s = "\\\\world\\\\";
unescape_string(s, true, '\\', '\"', false);
TS_ASSERT_EQUALS(s, "\\world\\");

s = "\\";
unescape_string(s, true, '\\', '\"', false);
TS_ASSERT_EQUALS(s, "\\");
```
In the code snippet, `s` is a string that undergoes unescaping based on the specified rules. The `unescape_string` function is called with the string `s` and the boolean parameters `true, '\\', '\"', false`, indicating that backslashes and double quotes should be unescaped, while forward slashes should not be unescaped. After the function call, the value of `s` is checked to ensure it matches the expected unescaped string.

Implement the `unescape_string` function in C++ to achieve the desired unescaping behavior.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void unescape_string(std::string& s, bool unescape_backslash, char backslash_char, char double_quote_char, bool unescape_forward_slash) {
    size_t pos = 0;
    while ((pos = s.find('\\', pos)) != std::string::npos) {
        if (pos + 1 < s.length()) {
            if (s[pos + 1] == backslash_char && unescape_backslash) {
                s.replace(pos, 2, 1, backslash_char);
            } else if (s[pos + 1] == double_quote_char) {
                s.replace(pos, 2, 1, double_quote_char);
            } else if (s[pos + 1] == '/' && unescape_forward_slash) {
                s.replace(pos, 2, 1, '/');
            }
        }
        pos++;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::string s = "\"world\"";
    unescape_string(s, true, '\\', '\"', false);
    std::cout << s << std::endl;  // Output: "world"

    s = "\\\\world\\\\";
    unescape_string(s, true, '\\', '\"', false);
    std::cout << s << std::endl;  // Output: \world\

    s = "\\";
    unescape_string(s, true, '\\', '\"', false);
    std::cout << s << std::endl;  // Output: \

    return 0;
}
```
The `unescape_string` function iterates through the input string and replaces the escaped sequences based on the specified rules. The main function demonstrates the usage of the `unescape_string` function with different input strings and parameters, producing the expected unescaped output.

