You are tasked with implementing a matrix multiplication function in C++ using the given code snippet as a starting point. The code snippet initializes two matrices A and B, and then attempts to multiply them using a function `matrixMultiplication`. Your task is to complete the implementation of the `initializeMatrix` and `matrixMultiplication` functions to ensure that the matrix multiplication is performed correctly.

The `initializeMatrix` function takes a 2D vector of integers and initializes it with random values. The `matrixMultiplication` function takes two 2D vectors of integers as input and returns their product as a new 2D vector. If the dimensions of the input matrices are not suitable for multiplication, the function should throw an exception with an appropriate error message.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `initializeMatrix` and `matrixMultiplication` functions to ensure that the code snippet runs without errors and correctly multiplies the matrices A and B.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdlib>

typedef std::vector<std::vector<int>> row;

// Function to initialize a matrix with random values
std::vector<row> initializeMatrix(const std::vector<row>& matrix) {
    std::vector<row> result(matrix.size(), row(matrix[0].size()));
    for (size_t i = 0; i < matrix.size(); ++i) {
        for (size_t j = 0; j < matrix[0].size(); ++j) {
            result[i][j] = rand() % 10;  // Initialize with random values between 0 and 9
        }
    }
    return result;
}

// Function to perform matrix multiplication
std::vector<row> matrixMultiplication(const std::vector<row>& A, const std::vector<row>& B) {
    size_t m = A.size();
    size_t n = A[0].size();
    size_t p = B[0].size();

    if (n != B.size()) {
        throw "Matrix dimensions are not suitable for multiplication";
    }

    std::vector<row> C(m, row(p, 0));

    for (size_t i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        for (size_t j = 0; j < p; ++j) {
            for (size_t k = 0; k < n; ++k) {
                C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
            }
        }
    }

    return C;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<row> A(10, row(5));
    std::vector<row> B(5, row(5));
    std::vector<row> C;

    A = initializeMatrix(A);
    B = initializeMatrix(B);

    try {
        C = matrixMultiplication(A, B);
        // Print the result matrix C
        for (size_t i = 0; i < C.size(); ++i) {
            for (size_t j = 0; j < C[0].size(); ++j) {
                std::cout << C[i][j] << " ";
            }
            std::cout << std::endl;
        }
    } catch (const char* msg) {
        std::cerr << "Error: " << msg << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this solution, the `initializeMatrix` function initializes a given matrix with random values, and the `matrixMultiplication` function performs matrix multiplication and returns the result. The main function demonstrates the usage of these functions by initializing matrices A and B, performing matrix multiplication, and printing the result matrix C. If the dimensions of the input matrices are not suitable for multiplication, an exception is thrown with an appropriate error message.

